[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Database Performance Fundamentals for Performance Engineers"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Database performance is often the primary bottleneck in modern distributed applications. When conducting performance testing, understanding how databases execute queries, manage connections, and store data is crucial for identifying root causes of latency. This tutorial covers the three pillars of database performance: Indexing, Connection Pooling, and Query Execution Plans."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Database Indexing"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Indexes are data structures (typically B-Trees) that improve the speed of data retrieval operations on a database table at the cost of additional writes and storage space. Without an index, the database must perform a full table scan, reading every row to find the matching records."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"B-Tree Indexes: The default index type in most relational databases, ideal for equality and range queries (=, <, >, BETWEEN)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Composite Indexes: Indexes on multiple columns. Order matters significantly; the leftmost prefix rule applies."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Index Trade-offs: While reads become faster, INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations become slower because the indexes must also be updated."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_status ON users (email, status);"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Connection Pooling"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Opening a new database connection involves TCP handshakes, authentication, and memory allocation, which is an expensive process. Connection pooling maintains a cache of active database connections so they can be reused when future requests are required."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pool Size Tuning: If the pool is too small, threads will block waiting for an available connection. If it is too large, it can overwhelm the database server with concurrent sessions."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Connection Leaks: Failing to close connections or return them to the pool leads to pool exhaustion, causing application failures under load."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"HikariConfig config = new HikariConfig();
config.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb");
config.setMaximumPoolSize(20);
config.setMinimumIdle(5);
HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource(config);"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Query Execution Plans"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"A query execution plan is a roadmap detailing how the database engine will execute a specific SQL statement. Performance engineers must analyze these plans to identify bottlenecks like sequential scans, missing indexes, and expensive nested loops."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"EXPLAIN Command: Use EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) in PostgreSQL or EXPLAIN in MySQL to view the actual execution steps and costs."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cost Estimation: The database optimizer calculates costs based on statistics. Outdated statistics lead to suboptimal plans."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'test@example.com';"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion for Performance Testers"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"When executing load tests, monitor database metrics alongside application metrics. Look for high CPU utilization, disk I/O bottlenecks, and connection wait times. Combine proper indexing, appropriately sized connection pools, and optimized query plans to ensure your database scales efficiently under peak load."}]
